        "Water makes up about 70 percent of most cells, and its properties follow from polarity and hydrogen bonding. It is an excellent solvent for ions and polar molecules, so dissolved glucose and salts move freely in blood and sap. Its high specific heat capacity buffers cells against temperature swings, its high latent heat of vaporisation makes sweating and transpiration effective coolants, and cohesion between molecules pulls water up the xylem in an unbroken column. Ice floats because hydrogen bonds hold molecules apart below four degrees Celsius, one of the standard facts of {S}.",
