        "Two models describe enzyme action. The lock and key model of Fischer treats the active site as a rigid shape matching the substrate exactly, which explains specificity but not why some enzymes act on several related substrates. The induced fit model of Koshland proposes that the active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate, straining its bonds toward the transition state. Induced fit explains both the high specificity of enzymes and the stabilisation of reaction intermediates, and it is the accepted model in every {S} text.",
